# 🧠 Easy Memory Trick

```text
String Literal
        ↓
Can use existing pooled String

new String()
        ↓
Creates a new String object

== 
        ↓
Reference comparison

.equals()
        ↓
Content comparison
```

---

# 🔑 Final Takeaway

The String Pool is one of the reasons Java can efficiently reuse commonly used String values.

The important rule is:

> **`==` checks whether two references point to the same object, while `.equals()` checks whether two Strings have the same content.**

Even if `==` sometimes returns `true` for String literals, you should **not rely on it for content comparison**.

Use:

```java
"expectedValue".equals(actualValue);
```

for safe and reliable String comparisons.

